James inquired about the standard practice for identifying 1-in-100 year events from a distribution of simulated events. I confirmed that taking the 99th percentile of the distribution is the standard approach, as it represents the value below which 99% of simulations fall, thus categorizing events at this threshold as rare. I emphasized that the reliability of this estimate hinges on the model's robustness and the number of simulations conducted. Additionally, I suggested considering extreme value theory for a deeper understanding of low-probability events and invited further questions about interpreting the results or integrating them into risk assessments.
